One caveat that matters
rebuild.sh already cleans each arch before building — that's what the rm -rf …/build/arm64 lines at the top of every run do, and it's why --no-clean exists. So a separate clean step is only needed when you want the tree empty without rebuilding. To build everything from scratch:

sh


cd /Users/pramodkumar/Hack/WS/UIOX
./rebuild.sh --continue 2>&1 | tail -8
That's already a clean build of all four. There's no separate clean needed first.

And the reason the clean is fussy here
From the script's own header — make clean alone is unreliable on this tree because clean-subsys deletes 02_FwHal/build and 30_KIX/30_DSS/10_DI/build, while the umbrella's steps 1/5/6/7 swallow their own failures with 2>/dev/null || echo "…skipped". The umbrella then finishes with archives missing and the link fails on uiox_fw_stor_get / uiox_proc_init / phys_alloc_page.

That's precisely why rebuild.sh drives each layer directly instead. So the answer depends on what you want:

Empty tree, no build → make -C 30_KIX clean, or the rm -rf above
Clean rebuild of all four → ./rebuild.sh --continue on its own
Run the grep -n 'clean' rebuild.sh first — if a clean subcommand is already there, that's your command and neither alternative is needed.